Licensing
=========

Copyright 2026 Reuben A. Popp, KF0OWR.

This repository is the design of a 1500 W automated antenna tuner. It is open
hardware, and its firmware is free software:

  Hardware and design   CERN-OHL-W-2.0     everything outside fw/, except the
                                           third-party material listed below
  Firmware (fw/)        GPL-3.0-or-later

The full texts are in LICENSES/.


Hardware and design: CERN-OHL-W-2.0
-----------------------------------

This covers the KiCad boards and the scripts that generate them (kicad/), the
CAD models and drawings (cad/), the NEC models and their results (nec/), the
design log with its diagrams and PDFs (doc/), the earlier drafts (archive/) and
the top-level README.

  This source describes Open Hardware and is licensed under the CERN-OHL-W v2.
  You may redistribute and modify this source and make products using it under
  the terms of the CERN-OHL-W v2 (https://ohwr.org/cern_ohl_w_v2.txt).

  This source is distributed WITHOUT ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTY,
  INCLUDING OF MERCHANTABILITY, SATISFACTORY QUALITY AND FITNESS FOR A
  PARTICULAR PURPOSE. Please see the CERN-OHL-W v2 for applicable conditions.

  Source location: https://github.com/driador/antenna-tuner

  As per CERN-OHL-W v2 section 4.1, should You produce hardware based on this
  source, You must, where practicable, maintain the Source Location visible on
  the external case of the tuner or other products you make using this source.

The boards carry the license and the Source Location on their back silkscreen,
and every drawing sheet and schematic carries them in its title block.


Firmware: GPL-3.0-or-later
--------------------------

The firmware in fw/ is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify it
under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by the Free
Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or (at your option) any
later version. It is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but
WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or
FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See the GNU General Public License for more
details.

The firmware is a separate work. It meets the hardware only through interfaces
the design documents: the pin map in design log section 8B.0.4 (PINMAP in
kicad/board_control.py) and the buses and signals of the parts on it. That makes
it External Material under CERN-OHL-W v2 section 1.8, so it keeps its own
license (sections 3.3(d) and 4.2). The data it carries from the design, such as
the pin numbers and the tables generated from nec/, is licensed with it under
GPL-3.0-or-later.

The Teensy build links QNEthernet, which is AGPL-3.0-or-later. As GPLv3 section
13 allows, a built firmware image combines the two: the GPL still covers this
firmware's own code, and section 13 of the AGPL (offering the source to users
over a network) applies to the combination. The page the tuner serves links to
this source for that reason. The portable core in fw/core contains no AGPL code.


Third-party material
--------------------

- doc/coilcraft-worksheet-link-coil.pdf is Coilcraft's Inductor Design
  Worksheet (Document 321), filled in for this project. It belongs to
  Coilcraft, Inc. and is not covered by either license above.
- nec/data/61-Material-Fair-Rite.csv and nec/data/61-Material-B-vs-Pv-at-25C.csv
  are Fair-Rite Products Corp.'s published data for their #61 ferrite material,
  kept for the choke analyses. They are Fair-Rite's and are not covered by
  either license above.
- The drawing PDFs embed the osifont typeface, which is distributed under the
  GNU LGPL version 3 with the GPL font exception.
- The firmware's build-time dependencies (the Teensyduino core and libraries,
  and QNEthernet) are fetched by PlatformIO and keep their own licenses.
